es  your way you need to seize it and do as  much as you can with it but over time  you wind up with so much going on that  you know you still have only the same 24  hours per day every single day and once  a day is gone you never do the pack so  there's there's a lot of thinking  through while can I commit to this so so  for example I'm now the editor of  Stoicism today and by the at sign by the  time that they approached me and said  hey you know the guy who was gonna who  had been the editor we're thinking of  stepping down I know you've been  involved with this project in writings  you know some blog entries I'm doing  stoical week would you would you be  interested in being the editor we had to  talk about this and think about whether  I could afford the time commitment and  whether it would make sense whereas in  you know five years ago I would have  said yeah sign me right up there there  are quite a few things like in the time  period where we made that decision I  think there's about five other  different pitches and projects that  people brought to us that we looked at  and then said no that's that's not our  our thing that's not really a good fit  there's other things where you put in  time like for example you know I do  tutoring services  I'd been considering doing that for a  while Google helpouts was one of the I  was one of the people recruited for that  and it worked for a while then Google as  they often do phased out that that  product and I continued doing the  tutoring services on my own because I'd  already made some some commitments of  time and energy to that as part of what  I did so you know learning how to  prioritize learning how to say yes and  and and when to say no that's that's a  very important thing that took me quite  a while to learn along with that I think  goes another sort of psychological  factor which is being content with what  projects you currently have going and  not losing sight of the fact very easy  to do the positive good that is being  generated developed you know expanded  circulated provided by what it is that  you're doing because it's sort of like  that that you know notion of the sphere  of knowledge as it increases you're  constantly increasing the the surface  area as well and you're becoming more  and more aware of what you're not doing  what you could what else you could be  doing what you could be doing better  it's important to maintain perspective  and to you know see what you've actually  gotten done and what resources you have  generated and what people or  institutions or organizations you  actually have helped rather than just  simply looking at all the other  possibilities which are just  possibilities they're not actualities  that could have been done in their place  and the last thing I would say for me  probably the biggest learning curve and  this is you know I think fairly typical  for somebody coming from traditional  academia  has been getting used to engaging in  what we could call marketing right  actually sending out pitches to to  various places and saying hey you want  to bring me in to talk I can do this for  you I can do that for you engaging in  all of that sort of stuff academics in  general are pretty passive they're used  to well I'm going to get my my classes  and the school will fill them up with  whatever students and I'll just do what  I do and I'll you know continue doing my  journal writing sending out articles or  working on my book and if I have to do  some service I'll do that too but but I  probably won't do an awful lot of you  know seeking that sort of thing out well  you know when you're an entrepreneur  it's the entire flipside of that if  you're very passive well you're not  going to remain an entrepreneur lawn or  you're not going to grow at all so you  constantly have to be putting yourself  into a much more active mode and that  has taken quite a while for me I've been  helped you know been helped out quite a  bit by having Andy with me and behind me  and also in front of me because she's  kind of more of a model for for that  sort of thing like I said I'm more  process-oriented a little bit more  risk-averse a little bit more let's  let's get this part done before I can  move on to that but reason IO has been a  great catalyst for doing that that sort  of thing so let me bring this to a close  by saying that I'm really happy to have  been doing this for five years it's hard  to believe that five years have actually  passed and I think that you know I can't  imagine going back to a traditional  academic position I could see like you  know doing a visiting professorship for  a year giving lectures on the sorts of  things that I'm interested in doing if  if indeed that ever comes up as a  possibility but I can't imagine ever  like going back  the academic job market and being  content with the sort of things that  frankly you know the the Academy at this  time wants you to just accept and go  along with we're at a very interesting  time in history there you know the  Internet has opened up huge vistas for  us to fill up with content and our own  activities and so reason IO is our way  of being able to do that and be our own  bosses as we do that and and you know  for me I think what what's most  rewarding about it I mentioned you know  marketing and we could even talk about  salesmanship if you wanted to but it's  different when you're doing this kind of  work you know if you're having to sell  cars particularly used cars particularly  used cars that haven't been you know  kept up very well you got to do a lot of  selling you might even have to lie to  somebody once in a while when your  product is essentially the wisdom that  has been accumulated and communicated  for the last 2,500 years just for me and  within the Western tradition that's a  little bit different you've got a  product that does a lot of the selling  for itself provided I actually make it  accessible for people so if I can you  know explain Aristotle's Nicomachean  ethics to other people or Plato's  symposium or Nietzsche's birth of  tragedy or pick whatever else you want  in such a way that you know allows them  a productive contact with the text  Aristotle will do the rest of the  selling I don't need to because the  ideas are so robust and so so  interesting so I've got a great you know  great job and a great product not so not  entirely happy with my boss but that's  because my boss is me and and you know  you could always find some complaints  with that but jokes aside this is really  an important occasion for us and so I'm  really happy to have reached this point  and we've got some really great stuff  coming up classes you know a number of  them being offered this this fall a lot  of content being generated still going  on at the half-hour hagel project I'm  gonna be editing the stoicism today blog  for for quite a while doing a lot of  other work you know and I'm still doing  traditional academic you know  publication and research as well so five  years in very happy about that  and I think that's probably a good place  to close  you
 

